#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Broadcasting rules {#broadcasting-rules}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The PUBs aggregate elements from multiple arrays (observables and parameter "
"values) by following the same broadcasting rules as NumPy. This section "
"briefly summarizes those rules.  For a detailed explanation, see the [NumPy "
"broadcasting rules "
"documentation](https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/basics.broadcasting.html).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Rules:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Input arrays do not need to have the same number of dimensions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '  * '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The resulting array will have the same number of dimensions as the input "
"array with the largest dimension.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '  * '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The size of each dimension is the largest size of the corresponding "
"dimension.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '  * '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Missing dimensions are assumed to have size one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Shape comparisons start with the rightmost dimension and continue to the "
"left.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two dimensions are compatible if their sizes are equal or if one of them is "
"1.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Examples of array pairs that broadcast:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Examples of array pairs that do not broadcast:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`Estimator` returns one expectation value estimate for each element of the "
"broadcasted shape.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here are some examples of common patterns expressed in terms of array "
"broadcasting.  Their accompanying visual representation is shown in the "
"figure that follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Parameter value sets are represented by n x m arrays, and observable arrays "
"are represented by one or more single-column arrays. For each example in the"
" previous code, the parameter value sets are combined with their observable "
"array to create the resulting expectation value estimates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: ' - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Example 1*: (broadcast single observable) has a parameter value set that is"
" a 5x1 array and a 1x1 observables array.  The one item in the observables "
"array is combined with each item in the parameter value set to create a "
"single 5x1 array where each item is a combination of the original item in "
"the parameter value set with the item in the observables array.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: ' - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Example 2*: (zip) has a 5x1 parameter value set and a 5x1 observables "
"array.  The output is a 5x1 array where each item is a combination of the "
"nth item in the parameter value set with the nth item in the observables "
"array.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '  - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Example 3*: (outer/product) has a 1x6 parameter value set and a 4x1 "
"observables array.  Their combination results in a 4x6 array that is created"
" by combining each item in the parameter value set with *every* item in the "
"observables array, and thus each parameter value becomes an entire column in"
" the output.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '  - '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Example 4*: (Standard nd generalization) has a 3x6 parameter value set "
"array and two 3x1 observables array.  These combine to create two 3x6 output"
" arrays in a similar manner to the previous example.\n"
msgstr ""

